/* Strategic command: set the route to a named station or solar system.
 *
 * Success means the route panel carries markers — the command confirms its
 * work rather than trusting the click that did it. That is also the first
 * condition, which makes the command idempotent: a route already set is a
 * command already done, and a mission restarted mid-flight does not throw
 * away the route the ship is following.
 *
 * The route block of the info panel is opened first, and it is opened whether
 * or not a destination was given: everything downstream reads the route from
 * that block — is_route_set, the markers the ship is steered by, the exit
 * condition of the whole flight — and while it is collapsed a route that
 * exists looks exactly like a route that does not.
 *
 * The steps are the client's own order, learned the hard way: the magnifier is
 * a toggle, the panel keeps its region in the tree even when closed, and the
 * results window buries the wanted category under hundreds of character names
 * until the groups are collapsed.
 *
 * Each action below takes two names, the outward one and the return one, and
 * picks between them itself: leg.pick(destination, return_destination) reads
 * which leg the mission is on off the blackboard, the same way for all five,
 * so this tree does not need to know which leg is flying — only main.btree's
 * loop and legs_done decide that. */

-> set_route {
    ->* {
        set_route_show_route()

        ? {
            /* Already there: nothing to do. */
            is_route_set?()

            -> {
                /* Nothing to look for — the mission was given no destination. */
                set_route_has_destination?(destination, return_destination)
                @! is_session_change?()

                /* A memory sequence, and it has to be one. A fallback would end
                   at the first step that succeeds — opening the panel, and the
                   command would call itself done having typed nothing. A plain
                   sequence would re-run every step each tick, and two of these
                   steps undo each other: collapsing the groups is done when no
                   category is expanded, expanding one is done when a category
                   is, so the pair would fight forever. Memory keeps the finished
                   steps finished. */
                ->* {
                    set_route_open_search()
                    set_route_clear_field()
                    set_route_type_destination(destination, return_destination)
                    /* The destination is passed so that a search which
                       already shows the wanted row is left as it is: without
                       it this collapsed what the search had opened and the
                       next step opened it again. */
                    set_route_collapse_groups(destination, return_destination)
                    set_route_expand_category(destination_kind, return_kind)
                    /* Succeeds on the route appearing, not on the press
                       landing: between the two the client says nothing, so a
                       step that trusted the press would hand the flight a
                       route that was never set. It orders again while the
                       route is missing and the row is still there. */
                    set_route_choose(destination, return_destination)
                }
            }
        }

        /* The search has done its work; from here on it is only in the way.
           This sits outside the branch above on purpose: it runs on the
           "already set" path too, and so clears a window left open by an
           earlier mission that was stopped mid-search. */
        set_route_close_search()
    }
}
